  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/2/2a/Fantastic_Four_Vol_1_16.png",
  "plot_summary": "The Human Torch streaks across the sky on his way to FF headquarters to discover that his three teammates have been shrunk down to the size of toys.&nbsp; After they recover, they soon reveal it's been happening over the course of days to -- all four of them!&nbsp; They each relay their tales over the next two pages, where the theme is the same - each had suddenly been reduced in size without warning.&nbsp; Reed suspects only \"the astonishing Ant-Man\" might be able to provide an explanation.\n\nNearby ants overhear and relay this information to Ant-Man, who rushes to see the FF.&nbsp; Ant-Man consults with the FF and leaves a couple of drops of each of his fluids for Reed to study.&nbsp; When he departs, Reed wonders if Ant-Man himself could be responsible, to which Sue replies \"Oh <i>no</i>, Reed!&nbsp; He's much too <i>cute</i>!&nbsp; I'll bet if he were normal-sized...<i>sigh</i>\" The Thing is disgusted, particularly since she was \"moonin' over the Sub-Mariner for months!\"\n\nSoon, Reed visits Alicia's apartment where Ben is helping her clean.&nbsp; He gives Ben a sample of the partial cure he's been working on, and it changes him back to Ben Grimm.&nbsp; Alicia actually says \"Why do you <i>feel</i> so different?&nbsp; I love you so, that I don't want you to change!&nbsp; I don't ever want <i>anything</i> to change you!\"&nbsp; Ben tells Reed thanks, but asks him to stop trying to change <i>him</i> and instead try and make Alicia see again.\n\nThe four keep hearing a voice, warning of Dr. Doom, so Reed begins to suspect he's still alive.&nbsp; They'll go after him by taking a sample of Ant-Man's shrinking formula and soon find themselves \"in the micro-world of Doctor Doom!\"&nbsp; And sure enough, there he sits, on a throne no less!&nbsp; Doom manipulates a control, and the four shrink even farther, leaving them helpless before Doom, who then begins to monologue and explain to them how he got there...\n\nAfter he shrunk, he discovered himself \"in a peaceful, primitive, happy world!&nbsp; It was enough to make my <i>blood</i> boil!\" -- \"The <i>fools!</i>  They live in peace.&nbsp; They are contented and happy!&nbsp; But <i>I'll</i> soon put a stop to <i>that</i>!\"&nbsp; He gained the trust of the king, who made him court scientist, and well...you can guess the rest.&nbsp; He used his new gadgets to take over the place and started to toy with the FF from his micro-world.\n\nWhen the guards of the micro-world attempt to capture the FF, they fight back, but Doom eventually gasses them to sleep, and they later awaken \"in a dank dungeon\" with the king and princess of the realm.&nbsp; It was the princess' voice they kept hearing.&nbsp; She says Doom wants to marry her.&nbsp; If she refuses, Doom will contact \"the planet Tok\" and offer them a reward for joining him -- the FF as their slaves!\n\nThe terrible warriors there \"are feared thruout [sic] our universe\" -- huge creatures coming from a planet with gravity a dozen times stronger than the Earth -- \"and they are coming for -- <i>you</i>!\"&nbsp; She goes into detail about how they'll use each member of the FF, including that Sue will \"spend the rest of her days as a scullery maid.\"\n\nBut as the princess relays all this information, we learn that Ant-Man has returned to visit the FF and finds the evidence that they shrunk.&nbsp; He too shrinks even smaller to follow them, is also captured and brought before Doom, who plans to include him in his payment to the \"lizard men of Tok,\" who will be arriving very soon now.\n\nMeanwhile the FF use their powers together to find a way out, enlarge themselves back to normal (at least \"normal\" in terms of the micro-world), and invisible Sue frees Ant-Man while the rest of them head out to meet the Tok invaders.&nbsp; The Thing uses a tower to bat the invading ship back toward its home planet.&nbsp; Doom steps in to stop them, but Sue yanks the gun from his hands, resulting in Doom fleeing into a trap door to enlarge himself, heading back to the surface of his own world.\n\nThe FF and Ant-Man round up all the evil guards, and the king and princess are reinstalled.&nbsp; The day is saved.\n\nBut as the five of them return to the normal-sized world, Doom still awaits in the next issue!",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "This was <i>easily</i> the most disjointed issue of the FF to date, containing both solid character beats and abysmal plot holes (many of which I'll detail below).\n\n[ASSET: right | MysteriousLaughter | width=15%] The story takes its time getting underway.&nbsp; After Johnny rescues his three shrunken teammates and they regain their normal sizes, a mysterious voice (and accompanying laughter) announces to them that it's only the <i>beginning</i>.&nbsp; And yet, rather than begin a search of their headquarters to see who said that, the quartet simply stands around for more than two entire pages relating to each other how they've randomly started shrinking.\n[BR]\n[BR] I don't know about you, but if I'd just been told by a disembodied voice that I was \"puny before my power,\" I think I'd show a little more interest in finding out who was responsible!\n[BR]\n[BR] It's actually strange how <i>casually</i> they treat the warning -- almost as if a taunting voice in their own headquarters is just another Tuesday at the Baxter Building!\n\n[ASSET: left | AntManMeetsFF | width=12%] Having Reed suggest that they attempt to contact Ant-Man was sensible enough, and thanks to his creepy eavesdropping ants, the Astonishing Ant-Man suddenly appears without warning, and the FF's current dilemma is explained.&nbsp; To help, Ant-Man leaves a sample of his shrinking and growing formula for Reed to study.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nUp to this point, there isn't much to criticize.&nbsp; Having the FF meet Ant-Man is even a decent way for Stan and Jack to once again reinforce that the Marvel Universe is shared.&nbsp; The real problem is that, by this time, the book is already more than a third of the way over, and these combined sequences have consumed 8 full pages.\n\nBut even <i>then</i>, there was more.&nbsp; <i>Still</i> not worrying about the fact that this mysterious voice just threatened them, Ben heads over to Alicia's house to help with some spring cleaning, and Reed suddenly shows up and essentially forces a strange liquid down the Thing's throat, which temporarily transforms him back to Ben Grimm.\n\nWhile this is quite random, and comes as a complete surprise, given that current circumstances would seem to be more important than returning to the work of \"curing\" the Thing, I did at least laugh out loud at Ben's reaction to the taste of the serum.\n[ASSET: center | LatestSerum | width=67%]\n\n(<i>Seriously</i>, the nauseated look on his face and cry out for chocolate flavoring had me in stitches for several moments.&nbsp; <i>Hahahaha!</i>)&nbsp; Ben's ability to inject humor into even the strangest situations is one of the book's most reliable charms.&nbsp; And moments like these remind us that even in the middle of a crisis, Ben's personality is still the beating heart of the team -- grumbling, joking, and reacting with knee-jerk honesty.\n\nAnd then, in the best character beat we've seen in these pages for a while, Ben finally admits to Reed what has only been hinted at before -- that Alicia actually <i>prefers</i> him as the Thing, and on the spot (top of page 10), Ben asks Reed directly to stop trying to find a way to change him back to human form and instead devote his time to try and help Alicia see again.&nbsp; Even Reed seems taken aback by this at first, but he agrees.\n\nIt's a rare moment of vulnerability from Ben, and Reed's quiet acceptance shows just how much their friendship has matured since the earliest issues.&nbsp; It's a moment that feels earned -- a small but meaningful evolution in their dynamic that stands out amid the issue's chaos.\n\nLike I said, this is the first genuinely solid character development we've seen in this book for a good while.&nbsp; And incidentally, even though the narrative text of the pages continues to call Alicia things like \"Ben Grimm's blind friend,\" their shared dialogue includes endearing terms such as \"my darling\" and \"baby\" and even \"I love Alicia.\"&nbsp; (So in only eight issues after having first met, Alicia and Ben are clearly more than just friends.)\n\n[DIVIDER]\nBut now, the book is almost half over, and it was high time for Stan and Jack to get on with things, using the small disembodied voice at the top of page 10 that warns them to \"Flee for your lives!&nbsp; Beware of <i>Doctor Doom</i>!\" -- followed by a cry for help.&nbsp; And even then, the co-creators delay the actual core of the plot for yet another page, showing how both Johnny and Sue are also hearing the voice.&nbsp; It isn't until the top of page 12 that they <i>finally</i> shrink themselves using Ant-Man's serum.\n\nAt last, the story decides to move forward -- though it's remarkable how long it takes for the issue to reach the conflict promised on the cover.&nbsp; <i>Finally</i>, after 12 full pages, they come face-to-face with the villain on the cover!\n\n[ASSET: right | DoomsSeriousIssues | width=21%] Unfortunately, this is where the story goes south.&nbsp; Doom's requisite monologue indicates that when he arrived in the micro-world, he was disgusted to find the people of the place living happily, even saying \"It was enough to make my blood boil!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] Passages like this only make the reader scratch his/her head and acknowledge that Doom has <i>serious</i> issues!&nbsp; Seeing people happily living their lives causes him to be sick to his stomach?&nbsp; <i>Wow</i>!&nbsp; Doom's disgust at happiness feels less like villainy and more like a personal malfunction -- as though contentment itself is an affront to his worldview.\n\nRegardless, Doom's takeover of the micro-world came as no surprise to readers, and we can even excuse him for wanting to keep the king of the realm and his daughter prisoners to use as hostages to ensure the loyalty of the palace guards and the general populace.\n\nBut from the explanation on page 17 of the other things that Doom has done since taking them prisoner, essentially the rest of the way through the book, it is mostly utter nonsense.&nbsp; Below are some of the lowlights.\n\n[HANG0] Doom wants to marry the <i>princess</i>?&nbsp; Since when has Doom ever shown interest in having any kind of partner?&nbsp; I mean, the guy can't even stand to see people living happily -- it makes his blood boil!&nbsp; And he wants a <i>wife</i>?&nbsp; Does he have any idea of what this <i>entails</i>?&nbsp; (<i>Heh</i>)\n\n[ASSET: right | WarriorsOfTok | width=15%] [HANG0] Doom contacts the world of \"Tok,\" offering them \"a reward for joining him!\"&nbsp; That reward is to be the Fantastic Four!&nbsp; This planet of Tok comes completely out of nowhere.&nbsp; Is it a full-sized planet or something that only exists in the micro-world?&nbsp; Why should we expect them to have even <i>heard</i> of the Fantastic Four?\n[BR]\n[BR] Even if we acknowledge the possibility that Doom might have learned of their existence when he was heading out to space at the end of issue 6 (we already saw in issue 10's monologue that he met people from a different world), how is he able to <i>contact</i> them?\n[BR]\n[BR] Furthermore, how do they arrive from outer space [SPAN: severity=error; text=directly into the <i>micro</i>-world and not in the <i>regular</i> world], such that the Thing is able to bat them back into space on page 21?\n[BR]\n[BR] And even if we excuse <i>all of that</i>, why would \"the terrible warriors of Tok\" travel all the way to Earth to help Doom just to get 4 (or 5) slaves, one of which would be nothing more than a scullery maid?&nbsp; The text says they come from a planet \"with gravity a dozen times stronger than ours!\"&nbsp; How would the members of the FF be of any help on their world when only someone like the Thing might be able to function on a planet with gravity like that?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=Have they literally no other people to subjugate to get slaves from?]&nbsp; They <i>are</i> supposed to be \"terrible warriors\" after all!\n[BR]\n[BR] This is about as random and dumb an idea as I've been exposed to yet, almost as if Stan and Jack needed an invading force and grabbed the first idea within arm's reach, logic be damned.\n\n[HANG0] If Doom could have returned to the \"real\" world at the end of the issue, why hadn't he already availed himself of this technology before and simply returned?&nbsp; Why go to all this trouble to toy with the Fantastic Four from the micro-world and contact the \"Lizard Men\" from Tok?&nbsp; <i>Ugh!</i>\n[BR]\n[BR] Doom's ability to return at will makes his entire micro‑world scheme feel like needless theatrics -- <i>impressive</i> theatrics, perhaps, but theatrics all the same.\n\n[HANG0] And what <i>about</i> this micro-world?&nbsp; People live down there?&nbsp; Will we ever hear from them <i>again</i>?&nbsp; The issue treats the micro‑world as though it's a fully populated realm, but the narrative seems in a hurry to forget it once the FF leave -- treated like a disposable backdrop -- here for a moment, then quietly abandoned.\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Sigh!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | SueFreesAntMan | width=50%] Ant-Man eventually shows up and is captured, but by this time the FF have escaped, which allows Sue to give Ant-Man an assist while the rest of the team prepares to turn back the invading forces from Tok (who seem to have no problem finding their way to the micro-world that somehow exists on Earth).\n\nAnd once Doom realizes that the jig is up, it's already page 22, which means that the issue has to come to a close.&nbsp; He escapes back to the \"surface of our <i>own</i> world,\" planning to give the FF \"a reception they'll <i>never</i> forget!\"\n[ASSET: center | DoomEscapes | width=67%]\n\nFinally, have another look at that cover.&nbsp; We're promised the \"Return of Doctor Doom!\" calling him \"the world's most <i>amazing</i> super-villain!!\"&nbsp; But this is literally on sale at the same time as Spider-Man's Doctor Octopus, \"the world's most <i>dreaded</i> super-villain!\" (see [LINK:1963:ASM_3|ASM #3]).&nbsp; Who is more amazing or dreaded?&nbsp; And what's this?&nbsp; On the splash page, Doom is labeled \"the world's most <i>dangerous</i> super-villain!\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Wow!&nbsp; Come on, Marvel -- make a choice here!]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nDespite the cover, which shows both Ant-Man and Doctor Doom, the book is mostly one or the other.&nbsp; <i>Yes</i>, Doom and Ant-Man do meet, but it's very brief.\n\nThe storyline involving Dr. Doom himself really doesn't begin until page 12, even though it is hinted at earlier.&nbsp; And to emphasize once more, other than the fact that Reed and the rest of the FF seem to completely ignore the disembodied voice that threatens them in their own headquarters, I found the first half of the book more than competent, and the scene with Ben and Alicia even quite nice.\n\nBut from the moment Doom appears and begins his monologue, and even reveals himself so petty as to not being able to tolerate happiness and commitment, I was already turned off.&nbsp; To compound that pettiness with the abject silliness of the people of Tok was nothing less than agonizing reading, riddled with plot holes, making the second half a brutal read.\n\nIn the end, it was indeed a tale of two halves, and since the first did have an actually solid character (and the one excellent laugh-out-loud) moment, I elected to award this a 3.0 because the latter half demanded effort to get through.&nbsp; The Ben/Alicia dynamic, together with the comedy was more than just a <i>hint</i> of a good idea, which justifies placing it above a 2.5.\n\nIt seems Doom will be back in the next issue, so we can only hope his motivations are more grounded in reality - but that remains to be seen!\n\nAs for Ant-Man's guest appearance, it was competent, even if not a standout.&nbsp; He didn't actually provide much help other than to assist them with the shrinking formula, so his role was limited.&nbsp; But then again... this isn't his magazine, so maybe that was okay.&nbsp; His appearance felt more like a cameo than a collaboration -- a reminder that the Marvel Universe is shared, even if that sharing is sometimes superficial.\n\nAnt-Man's presence here is more symbolic than functional, a reminder that Marvel's heroes inhabit the same world even when their stories barely intersect.&nbsp; (And it's not at all as textured as Johnny's appearance in this month's <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i>, which actually affected the events.)",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] * This issue features a full-page pin-up of Mister Fantastic and explains about his powers and the way his costume molds to his body.\n\n[HANG0] * Cover Note:&nbsp; Ant-Man is advertised as the \"surprise\" guest star, but given that his presence was being touted as part of last issue, I'm not sure how anyone could have been surprised to find he'd be involved here.&nbsp; Plus, at the end of [LINK:1962:FF_10|FF #10], we saw Dr. Doom shrinking away to nothingness, so shrinking ability?&nbsp; It only makes sense.&nbsp; <i>Ah well</i>...\n\n[HANG0] * The middle panel on page 2 shows the Baxter Building with <b>six</b> upper floors (instead of five), showing once again that even after a year and a half of drawing FF issues, Jack still can't quite keep track of this.\n\n[HANG0] * Ben mistakenly calls Alicia \"Sue baby\" at the bottom of page 9, right in front of Reed, no less!&nbsp; <b><i>Oops!</i></b>\n\n[HANG0] * The editorial note at the bottom of page 12 mentions that the last encounter the FF had with Dr. Doom took place in [LINK:1962:FF_10|FF #10] (which is correct), but it lists the date as \"Sept.\"&nbsp; This can't be correct because [LINK:1962:FF_10|FF #10] was released in October 1962 with a cover date of January 1963, <i>neither</i> of which is September.&nbsp; (I guess Stan needs to work on his editorial skills.)\n\n[HANG0] * Johnny and Princess Pearla seem to hit it off, even to the point where she suggests that Johnny stay behind.&nbsp; What is it with Johnny and young women from other realms?&nbsp; This <i>just happened</i> to him only a few months back with Valeria in the 5<sup>th</sup> dimension, back in [LINK:1962:ST_103_torch0|ST #103]."
  ],
  "letters_page": "Bill DeAndrew was surprised by the fact his parents recognized the Sub-Mariner from his pin-up in [LINK:1962:FF_11_ff1|FF #11], telling him he appeared in comics <i>before</i> the Fantastic Four.&nbsp; Marvel assures him that his parents are correct!\n\nTom Dietz expresses his criticism of Steve Ditko's art in [LINK:1962:ASM_1_asm1|ASM #1], in comparison to his earlier work in <i>Amazing Fantasy</i>, calling the art \"horrid.\"&nbsp; He then adds \"(If someone slits my throat, we have a suspect.)\"&nbsp; <i>Heh</i>, this made me laugh out loud.\n\nDavid Singer from Rochester, NY concludes the letter page by \"hoping the FF, Ant-Man, Spider-Man, Thor and Iron Man are published till my dying day -- which I hope will be a long time from now.\"&nbsp; (Just for fun, I tried to find out if Mr. Singer was still around, and encouragingly, I can find no record of any obituary, so that bodes well for the magazine -- keep hanging in there Dave!&nbsp; Our heroes need you!)",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}